# Conventional commits (auto)

When this repo has a coherent, reviewable unit of work ready — and enough change to justify an atomic commit — **create the git commit without waiting to be asked**.

## When to commit

- A feature, fix, refactor, test, or chore is functionally complete
- The diff is one logical change (or split into multiple atomic commits)
- Do **not** leave large finished scoring/auth/UI batches uncommitted across turns

## When not to commit

- Work is mid-flight / broken / untested
- Only exploratory local scripts, scratch files, or secrets
- User explicitly said not to commit

## Message format (Conventional Commits)

```
type(scope): short imperative summary
```

- **Types:** `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `docs`, `style`, `perf`
- **Scope:** area of the app (`scoring`, `auth`, `predict`, `admin`, `i18n`, …)
- Summary: why/what in ~72 chars; match existing `git log` tone
- Body optional for non-obvious rationale

Examples from this repo: `feat(login): Update backsplash`, `fix(app): Update copy`

## Safety (still apply)

- Never update git config
- Never commit `.env`, credentials, or production secrets
- Never force-push to main, amend unless the usual amend conditions hold
- Prefer **not** committing one-off `scripts/local-*` debug helpers unless the user asks
- Follow the existing prediction-data rule: never wipe prediction history in commits that add "cleanup" migrations

## Procedure

1. `git status` / `git diff` / recent `git log` for style
2. Stage only files for that atomic unit
3. Commit with HEREDOC message
4. `git status` to verify
